{
  "image_name": "d00061cd80c7aa4af358474c17b4b34d.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a drug-discovery screening workflow and highlights how interference and false positives are filtered out from start to finish. It follows compounds from internal or external sources into a screening deck, then through assay design and anti-interference measures into a primary screening assay. Compounds that are inactive are dropped early, while active ones move forward for further testing.\n\nThe central message is that both computational and experimental safeguards are built into every stage. Across the top, the figure shows a timeline from screening campaign development, through compound screening, to hit triage and compound progression. Theoretical strategies include rule sets for rejecting undesirable compounds, predictors of assay interference, online monitoring, anomaly detection from dose–response and structure–activity relationships, and comparison of measurements with predictions. A computer icon suggests data-driven or machine-learning support during screening.\n\nExperimentally, active primary hits are checked in counter-screens and orthogonal assays, sometimes using multiple assays, plus additional vetting and purity checks. Depending on these outcomes, compounds are either dropped, cleared of concern, or progressed. Overall, the diagram emphasizes a layered decision process designed to identify true active compounds while removing assay artifacts and problematic hits.",
